{
  "requirement": "Plain text defines no attachments. The file root and each `line` item have\nempty attachment lists, including lines that begin with comment delimiters\nfrom another language.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Obligation.** For plain text, the file root and every `line` item must have empty attachment lists. That includes lines that begin with comment delimiters from another language.\n\n**Test.** `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/outline_content.rs#fn[bundled_languages_classify_only_their_attachment_syntax]` carries a `{test}` reference to `requirements.sdk.sources.plain-text.attachments`. Its `test.txt` case loads `\"# just text\\n\"`. `#` is a Python/TOML/YAML comment delimiter, so this is a line beginning with another language's comment delimiter.\n\n**Assertion.** The loop flattens the attachments of every item in `snapshot.outline().items` and asserts the result equals `vec![]`.\n- Other tests in the same file show that `items[0]` is the root with an empty selector.\n- They also show that the `line` items follow it. See `unknown_extensions_keep_nonblank_lines_and_content_selectors` and `unicode_spans_use_bytes_and_whitespace_only_lines_create_no_items`.\n- So the flat_map covers both the root and the line item, and a `#` line that picked up a comment attachment would fail the assertion.\n\n**Possible gaps.**\n- The test only uses `#`. Other delimiters such as `//`, `/* */` and `<!--` are not tried. The implementation, `fn[plain_text]` in `src/outline/mod.rs`, gives plain text empty `comments` and `annotations` unconditionally, with no per-delimiter logic. A missed delimiter would therefore need a separate classifier for plain text, and that is not a distinct input class the requirement singles out.\n- The assertion is vacuous if no items exist, but other tests pin the plain-text item structure.\n\nNo gap found.",
  "references": "1ef306a66d16e7b2dae06e3da1cb46df121252836c2196dd6eda170dff068010",
